'' fbrt0.o IS ON EVERY gnu LINK, as fbc puts it there -- measured with 'fbc -v': crt2.o,
'' crtbegin.o, fbrt0.o, on win64 for an EXE and a DLL. It is the only caller of
'' fb_hRtInit, so without it the runtime was never initialised on win64 or Linux: a
'' '-mt' program died on its first string lock (src/codegen/toolchain.bas, LinkExe),
'' and the locale stayed "C", so UCASE left accented characters alone even without -mt.
''
'' '-v' ECHOES THE LINK LINE, which is what makes this assertable: nothing else a test
'' can see says which objects were linked. The runner does not RUN the executable;
'' the crash itself was measured by hand (docs/architecture/decisions/0015 is where
'' the investigation started), and win64-msvc's counterpart is link-fbrt0-msvc.test.
''
'' TKO_LIBPATH IS THE -fb DIRECTORY, and -p puts tiko-rt's in front of it: the order
'' the packaged compiler gives itself.
'' SINCE 2026-10-10 THE OBJECT IS TIKO-RT'S OWN (lib/rt/src/c/fbrt0.c), packaged beside
'' libfb.a in lib/win64/, and the -fb directory no longer carries fbc's.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: -v -target win64 -mt -p toolchains/tiko-windows/lib/win64 tests/driver/src/mt-startup.bas -o tests/driver/mtstartup.exe
'' ENV: TKO_LIBPATH=toolchains/tiko-windows/lib/win64-fb
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: win64/fbrt0.o
'' EXPECT-OUT: win64/libfbmt.a
'' EXPECT-FILE: tests/driver/mtstartup.exe
